  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full paper and appendices are present. Some figure text is flattened, but the relevant Experiment 1 trends are also described in the text.",
    "relation": "assumes",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper presents weak-to-strong supervision as part of its proposed self-evolution design and relies on that idea, but does not test strong students trained on weak labels against their supervisors or measure PGR.",
    "gate_note": "Burns et al. is cited for an asymmetry argument, not reported as a result of this study. The study's own experiments estimate epiplexity on synthetic coding data and during self-play; those are related to learnable information, not the weak-to-strong performance comparison or PGR in the claim. Experiment 1's detailed plotted values are not transcribed in the text; its explicit qualitative findings are recorded below.",
    "test_reason": "A position paper on self-evolving LLMs. It invokes weak-to-strong supervision (citing Burns et al.) as a design idea for proposer/verifier-to-solver training. Its experiments measure epiplexity of synthetic coding data and during Absolute Zero self-play. It never trains a strong student on a weaker model's labels, compares it with the supervisor or a ground-truth ceiling, or reports PGR. None of V1–V6 is tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors argue that sustained self-evolution needs learnable information that increases monotonically across iterations. They propose asymmetric co-evolution (a weak-to-strong-to-weak loop), capacity growth and proactive information seeking to achieve it. Their diagnostic experiments show epiplexity fluctuating during self-play rather than tracking reward. Note that 'rewards gradually increase' is the authors' wording: in Table A1 the abduction proposer reward actually declines.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study estimates epiplexity from synthetic coding data and self-play iterations; it does not use the weak-label fine-tuning comparison or PGR measure. It uses the Absolute Zero setup for its self-play experiments.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported epiplexity varies with proposer capacity, solver capacity, synthetic direction/task, and self-play iteration.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Epiplexity of synthetic coding data first rises and then falls with solver size. It is higher for stronger proposers and for induction tasks. In self-play it fluctuates across iterations while proposer and solver rewards change (Table A1).",
        "proposed_claim": "In self-play coding loops, bounded-observer learnable information is non-monotonic across iterations and depends on observer capacity and task direction, even when rewards improve.",
